    "body": "## Overview\n\nReport a missing, null or non-string `url` in a `vllm run-batch` input line as `Invalid request on line N: ...` instead of a raw `KeyError: 'url'`.\n\n## Claims\n\n- `validate_batch` now rejects a bad `url` with its normal per-line error, before the model loads.\n\n## Validation\n\n`pytest tests/entrypoints/launchers/test_run_batch.py -k validate_batch` (CPU, no GPU needed): the new `test_validate_batch_reports_a_bad_url_field` fails before (3 failed, `KeyError: 'url'` at `run_batch.py:188`) and passes after (5 passed). `ruff check` and `ruff format` are clean.\n\n## Details\n\n`BatchRequestInput.check_type_for_url` reads `info.data[\"url\"]`. Pydantic still runs the `body` validator after `url` fails, so the key is absent, and `validate_batch` only catches `pydantic.ValidationError`. The fix uses `info.data.get(\"url\")` and returns the body untouched when `url` is `None`, so the `url` error is the one reported. No method check or other validation is added.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
